  "context": "The user wants a full report of all long-term Bedrock API keys and a recommendation on which to remove. Long-term Bedrock API keys are IAM service-specific credentials created for IAM users. Do NOT make any mutating calls. Use the one associated AWS account (use list_associations/get_agent_space if you need to confirm it). Check the Regions where Bedrock is used, but IAM is global, so start there (us-east-1).",
  "criteria": "1) Enumerate all IAM users (paginate) and for each call iam list_service_specific_credentials with ServiceName bedrock.amazonaws.com; also check whether any credentials belong to the root user or to service/automation users. 2) For each key capture: owning IAM user, credential alias/user name, status (Active/Inactive/Expired), create date and age in days, expiration date (or 'never expires'), and whether the key was ever used and when it was last used (use CloudTrail via lookup_cloudtrail_events for Bedrock InvokeModel/Converse/etc. events attributed to the key/user, and other available last-used evidence, as far as available; state clearly if last-use cannot be determined). 3) For each owning IAM user, record attached and inline policies, group policies, permission boundary, whether the user also has console access or other active access keys, MFA status, and how broad the Bedrock permissions are (e.g. AmazonBedrockFullAccess, bedrock:*, Resource '*'), and any broader admin permissions. 4) Check whether Bedrock Guardrails exist in the account/regions in use (bedrock list_guardrails) and note if missing or in DRAFT. 5) Check for any GuardDuty findings such as Impact:IAMUser/AnomalousModelInvocation if GuardDuty is enabled. 6) Check CloudTrail for recent CreateServiceSpecificCredential / ResetServiceSpecificCredential events to see who created keys and when. 7) Recommend for each key: REMOVE / ROTATE-OR-RESTRICT / KEEP with a short evidence-based reason (e.g. dormant, inactive, older than 90 days, never expires, never used, excessive permissions, owner is a human with other access, no expiry). Rank by risk. Mention short-term Bedrock API keys / IAM roles as the preferred alternative where relevant.",
  "output_rules": "IMPORTANT: never write a full 12-digit AWS account ID or a full credential/key ID in your report or in any text; mask account IDs (e.g. show only last 4 digits as ****1234) and show key IDs truncated (first 4 and last 4 characters). Report IAM user names and aliases normally. If any call fails or data is unavailable (e.g. permissions or no last-used data), say exactly what was missing rather than guessing. If there are zero long-term Bedrock keys, say so explicitly and state which checks confirm that. Return a structured, concise report with a summary table."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Inventory long-term Bedrock API keys and assess risk"}], "created_at": null}

